WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.358
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.358 --> 00:00:08.685
The girls quietly climbed in the van.

00:00:08.685 --> 00:00:11.236
Amy slammed the trunk close and

00:00:11.236 --> 00:00:17.327
started the three day drive from
Washington DC back to the Mountain West.

00:00:17.327 --> 00:00:21.292
She had done it, she had finally left him.

00:00:21.292 --> 00:00:28.261
After five years of living in constant
fear that he might in some way hurt her or

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:32.241
her three girls, she had finally left him.

00:00:32.241 --> 00:00:34.815
She thought about
the challenges that lay ahead.

00:00:34.815 --> 00:00:39.582
There had been emotional and
psychological trauma for

00:00:39.582 --> 00:00:43.938
her and her daughters Kira,
Jessie, and Gabby.

00:00:43.938 --> 00:00:48.625
But it was worth it to be
able to raise her three girls

00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:52.016
away from the addictions and abuse.

00:00:52.016 --> 00:00:55.296
She had been to college,
but it had been four and

00:00:55.296 --> 00:01:00.721
a half years since she had last had a job
and she never actually used her degree.

00:01:00.721 --> 00:01:03.591
Amy qualified for
food stamps and Medicaid.

00:01:03.591 --> 00:01:09.001
These government programs were
a lifeline but they were also a crutch.

00:01:09.001 --> 00:01:14.115
To continue to qualify,
she had to stay in a low paying job.

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:18.801
There was nothing to help her
transition off of the government

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:21.063
assistance to a better life.

00:01:21.063 --> 00:01:22.440
Amy was stuck.

00:01:24.592 --> 00:01:28.867
Amy is not alone in her struggles
as a single mother in this country,

00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:33.001
there are over 11 million single
parents in the United States.

00:01:33.001 --> 00:01:36.312
Since over 80% of these
are single mothers,

00:01:36.312 --> 00:01:39.303
my talk is going to focus
on their struggles.

00:01:39.303 --> 00:01:44.086
Of course, everything that I
shared today applies to all

00:01:44.086 --> 00:01:47.486
single parents, mothers and fathers.

00:01:47.486 --> 00:01:51.050
So you're probably thinking Dave,
you're not a single parent.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.148
Why are you talking about this?

00:01:53.148 --> 00:01:58.854
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because Amy is my fiance.

00:01:58.854 --> 00:02:02.000
We first met when we were 18 years old.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.141
We then went our separate ways for
12 years.

00:02:05.141 --> 00:02:10.926
During that time, he got into a bad
marriage and finally left four years ago.

00:02:10.926 --> 00:02:14.421
Mutual friends brought us
back together two years ago.

00:02:14.421 --> 00:02:21.295
Finally this last Christmas,
I asked Amy to marry me.

00:02:21.295 --> 00:02:26.674
Because this relationship was
more than just her and me,

00:02:26.674 --> 00:02:32.740
I wanted Kiera, Gabi, and
Jessie to be there for the proposal.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:36.737
Then one at a time,
I got down on one knee,

00:02:36.737 --> 00:02:41.192
put a little ring on each
of their fingers, and

00:02:41.192 --> 00:02:44.518
asked them if I could be their dad.

00:02:44.518 --> 00:02:46.012
They said yes!

00:02:46.012 --> 00:02:55.078
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:02:55.078 --> 00:02:58.156
&gt;&gt; I am the luckiest man in the world

00:02:58.156 --> 00:03:01.924
to have these amazing girls in my life.

00:03:01.924 --> 00:03:05.961
Hearing about Amy's struggles
as a single mother has been so

00:03:05.961 --> 00:03:09.692
frustrating because there's so
much that can be done.

00:03:09.692 --> 00:03:14.792
For this talk, I'm going to share all
that I've learned about why single

00:03:14.792 --> 00:03:21.092
mothers struggle to escape poverty and how
college and childcare assistance can help.

00:03:21.092 --> 00:03:25.220
There are four key numbers you
should remember from this talk.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.623
The first is 1 in 4.

00:03:28.623 --> 00:03:34.090
One in four children in the United States
is raised by a single mother.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:39.150
This is in large part due to
the growing trend of children being

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:45.198
born out of wedlock, divorce, and
abandonment due to substance abuse.

00:03:45.198 --> 00:03:49.752
More than a third of single mother
households live in poverty.

00:03:49.752 --> 00:03:56.130
For the second key number, $1.9 billion.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:03.907
The government provides $1.9 billion in
food stumps every year to single mothers.

00:04:03.907 --> 00:04:07.778
That's a market size that make
any eventual capitalist true.

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:12.774
These government programs do so

00:04:12.774 --> 00:04:16.621
much to help these single
mother get back on their feet.

00:04:16.621 --> 00:04:21.477
Unfortunately, even though they
are intended to be temporary,

00:04:21.477 --> 00:04:25.551
there are no incentives to
help people get off of them.

00:04:25.551 --> 00:04:33.235
So I've leaned college is critical to
help the single mothers escape poverty.

00:04:33.235 --> 00:04:34.211
The Institute for

00:04:34.211 --> 00:04:38.122
Women's Policy Research has done
a ton of research on single mothers.

00:04:38.122 --> 00:04:41.550
They have shown single mothers
with a college degree,

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:46.335
get significantly better jobs,
make more money, and have better health.

00:04:46.335 --> 00:04:50.945
And all these benefits trickle
down to their children as well.

00:04:50.945 --> 00:04:55.852
Now for the third key number, $30,000.

00:04:55.852 --> 00:05:00.537
$30,000 is the average net
gain the government can get if

00:05:00.537 --> 00:05:04.975
they were to help a single
mother earn a bachelor's degree.

00:05:04.975 --> 00:05:08.866
If the government ought to
provide childcare assistance,

00:05:08.866 --> 00:05:12.598
financial aid, and
a case worker to the single mothers,

00:05:12.598 --> 00:05:17.421
ultimately, they would come out ahead
in the additional tax benefits and

00:05:17.421 --> 00:05:21.571
tax revenue from the higher paying job for
that single mother.

00:05:21.571 --> 00:05:25.689
$30,000, and
that's not even considering all

00:05:25.689 --> 00:05:30.430
the benefits that come to
the children of these single mothers.

00:05:31.649 --> 00:05:34.860
The last piece of this
puzzle is childcare.

00:05:36.257 --> 00:05:39.773
For a mother to be able
to go back to school,

00:05:39.773 --> 00:05:44.975
she needs help raising her kids,
but childcare is expensive.

00:05:44.975 --> 00:05:45.951
On average,

00:05:45.951 --> 00:05:51.463
a single mother will spend a quarter
to a third of her income on childcare.

00:05:51.463 --> 00:05:55.685
The government used to provide childcare
assistance to single mothers while they're

00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:56.328
in college.

00:05:56.328 --> 00:05:59.434
But during the welfare
reforms of the mid 90s,

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:04.003
the government stopped providing
childcare assistance to students.

00:06:04.003 --> 00:06:07.702
Enrollment of single mothers
in college plummeted.

00:06:11.027 --> 00:06:15.613
For those single mothers who have
access to childcare assistance,

00:06:15.613 --> 00:06:19.000
their likelihood of
graduating on time triples.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.231
Now for the final key number, 49%.

00:06:23.231 --> 00:06:29.361
Only 49% of college campuses have
childcare centers on campus.

00:06:29.361 --> 00:06:33.496
There's a government program that helps
support these child care centers, but

00:06:33.496 --> 00:06:35.700
it's obviously not nearly large enough.

00:06:37.336 --> 00:06:41.049
Now that I've shared these
startling statistics with you,

00:06:41.049 --> 00:06:42.441
we need to act on this.

00:06:45.524 --> 00:06:48.744
Let's think about the single
mothers in our life, and

00:06:48.744 --> 00:06:52.179
make sure that they are welcomed
on every college campus.

00:06:52.179 --> 00:06:56.127
Every college campus should have
a childcare center on campus.

00:06:56.127 --> 00:06:59.450
Do you know if the GSB has childcare?

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:04.779
If not, ask the administration so
that they know you care.

00:07:04.779 --> 00:07:11.463
On top of the GSB, Stanford has
childcare for both students and faculty.

00:07:11.463 --> 00:07:15.535
For colleges that are so
concerned about diversity and inclusion,

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:19.394
by not making it possible for
single mothers to attend college,

00:07:19.394 --> 00:07:22.696
they are missing out on this
key group of individuals.

00:07:22.696 --> 00:07:27.439
As a side note, we as future business
leaders need to make sure it's

00:07:27.439 --> 00:07:31.914
feasible for single mothers
to join us in the workplace.

00:07:31.914 --> 00:07:34.999
Second, support the single
mothers in your life.

00:07:34.999 --> 00:07:40.340
These amazing women have done so
much to raise children and

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:44.489
taking on the parenting
roles of two people.

00:07:44.489 --> 00:07:48.132
Talk with them about those struggles and
ask how you can help.

00:07:48.132 --> 00:07:51.702
Finally, there needs to be
a government policy change.

00:07:51.702 --> 00:07:55.125
The government should provide
childcare assistance and

00:07:55.125 --> 00:07:58.623
other support to every single
mother that's in college.

00:07:58.623 --> 00:08:02.699
This financially and socially make sense.

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:07.978
So in conclusion, remember,
there are four key numbers,

00:08:07.978 --> 00:08:13.480
1 in 4, $1.9 billion, $30,000, and 49%.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:17.637
That 49% should be 100%.

00:08:20.206 --> 00:08:23.863
So what's the rest of Amy's story?

00:08:23.863 --> 00:08:27.905
Amy is getting her masters
in public administration and

00:08:27.905 --> 00:08:31.780
is working for
a government city management office.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.660
She didn't have access to
childcare through her college but

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:39.166
luckily her parents helped
her take care of her girls.

00:08:39.166 --> 00:08:44.319
Amy wants to help undeserved single
mothers gain access to college and

00:08:44.319 --> 00:08:45.374
a childcare.

00:08:45.374 --> 00:08:48.854
She and
I are working on a non-profit to push for

00:08:48.854 --> 00:08:53.640
these initiatives as well as push for
government policy change.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:57.127
In her words,
I don't see this as a hand out but

00:08:57.127 --> 00:09:01.966
as a hand up so that single mothers
are on an equal playing field.

00:09:01.966 --> 00:09:07.844
Single mothers like Amy are raising
many of the future generation and

00:09:07.844 --> 00:09:10.285
they deserve a fair chance.

00:09:10.285 --> 00:09:11.179
Thank you.

00:09:11.179 --> 00:09:16.250
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:18.207
[MUSIC]

